# Inspection Space - Interaction Model

## Purpose

Inspection Space gives the user access to a Brique element:

* directly from another space;
* through a semantic-navigation result;
* through a textual-search result.

Inspection Space is an access and reading space. It does not edit Brique
files directly. Opening the corresponding source file delegates editing to
VS Code.

## Access Modes

Inspection Space has three modes:

```text
Inspect
Browse
Search
```

Each mode represents a different way to access a Brique element.

## Inspect Mode

Inspect Mode displays the currently opened Brique element.

The element may be a Context, Capacity, Matter, Schema, Document, Trace, or any
other Brique element.

The display is oriented toward reading:

* information is presented as readable semantic sections rather than raw JSON;
* Brique, Objective, Subjective, and Functional are the four expandable
  sections;
* the four sections are expanded by default;
* content inside a section is displayed as a simple textual key-value list;
* nested object and list keys are expandable, but collapsed by default;
* each object key uses its disclosure triangle for Expand One Level and a
  second downward-arrow icon immediately beside it for Expand All;
* Expand All is available only when the key contains a deeper nested subtree.

Inspection preserves the user's reading orientation when the inspected element
changes:

* opened sections remain opened;
* the first opened key inside a section remains opened;
* deeper opened paths and recursive expansion are not preserved.

Expand One Level reveals only the direct children of the selected key. Nested
subtrees remain collapsed. Expand All recursively reveals the complete subtree
of the selected key. Activating Expand All again collapses the recursive
subtrees while keeping the selected key open at one level.

The user can open the source file corresponding to the inspected element in
VS Code.

## Browse Mode

Browse Mode lets the user navigate the semantic field.

The semantic field is composed of categories and values. The user progressively
selects categories and values to qualify the elements being sought.

Every selection change updates the result list. Results are Brique elements
that satisfy the current semantic criteria.

Opening a result temporarily switches Inspection Space to Inspect Mode.

## Search Mode

Search Mode provides textual search over the names and identifiers of Brique
elements.

Changing the textual query updates the result list.

Opening a result temporarily switches Inspection Space to Inspect Mode.

## Inspection Navigation

Inspection Space preserves the exploration state from which an element was
opened:

```text
Browse -> Result -> Inspect
Search -> Result -> Inspect
```

The user can return from temporary Inspect Mode to the originating exploration.

Returning to Browse Mode restores:

* selected categories;
* selected values;
* results;
* scroll position.

Returning to Search Mode restores:

* textual query;
* results;
* scroll position.

Inspecting a result therefore does not interrupt the current exploration
context.

## Principles

```text
Inspect  See an element.
Browse   Find an element through semantic navigation.
Search   Find an element through textual search.
```

Inspection Space provides access to and readable inspection of Brique
elements. Editing their real files belongs to VS Code.
